#{[{:activity/id #uuid "08de5d22-7723-5283-ac76-b0a9c54b4ea9" :activity/steps [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186088497 :step/content {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186088850 :tutorial/content "We can draw a curve by defining a relationship between $x$ and $y$ using a third parameter $t$. For example, suppose we have\n\n$$\nx=2t, \\,\\,\\, y=t^2, \\quad -3\\leq t\\leq 3\n$$\n\nThese are the **parametric equations** of the curve.\n\nFirst, let's create a table for some values of $t$:\n\n| **$t$** | $-3$ | $-2$ | $-1$ | $0$ | $1$ | $2$ | $3$ |\n| --- | ---: | ---: | ---: | ---: | ---: | ---: | ---: |\n| $x= 2t$ | $-6$ | $-4$ | $-2$ | $0$ | $2$ | $4$ | $6$ |\n| $y=t^2$ | $9$ | $4$ | $1$ | $0$ | $1$ | $4$ | $9$ |\n\nPlotting these points, we get the following curve:\n\n![](/home/jake/Developer/MA/DATA/Lessons/803/Source/Images/13a7b7e8ef810bb0af302b19de45175d.png)\n\nThe curve is actually a parabola.\nA curve defined by parametric equations is called a **parametric curve** and is said to be \"defined parametrically\"." :tutorial/id #uuid "f1327a56-c215-50cb-a30e-e43369078a3c" :tutorial/math-academy-id 3349 :tutorial/title "Introduction"} :step/id #uuid "218377cb-122a-56d3-9bc2-df2b7ebad88e" :step/math-academy-id 18547 :step/next {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186088498}} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186088498 :step/content {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186087654 :knowledge-point/canonical-example {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186087453} :knowledge-point/id #uuid "bbf50e27-a8c3-52e7-be21-5d7ed61e5512" :knowledge-point/key-prerequisites [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186050774}] :knowledge-point/questions [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186088211} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186088212}] :knowledge-point/title "Filling a Table of Values for a Parametric Curve"} :step/id #uuid "100ba475-df33-5ce4-9f19-dc90b51a15ac" :step/math-academy-id 18548 :step/next {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186088503}} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186088503 :step/content {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186087658 :knowledge-point/canonical-example {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186087457} :knowledge-point/id #uuid "e0dc11d5-8eda-52b1-b7b1-42b481813ff4" :knowledge-point/key-prerequisites [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186049056}] :knowledge-point/questions [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186087968} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186087969}] :knowledge-point/title "Graphing a Curve Defined Parametrically"} :step/id #uuid "5621dae4-d9c7-5199-b4f0-2ee503009aa1" :step/math-academy-id 18603 :step/next {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186088504}} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186088504 :step/content {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186087659 :knowledge-point/canonical-example {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186087458} :knowledge-point/id #uuid "3cbf0ad0-a7c5-5c0f-a06c-9210fdc244ef" :knowledge-point/key-prerequisites [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186050356} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186050564} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186050626}] :knowledge-point/questions [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186087971} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186087972}] :knowledge-point/title "Determining the Range of a Parametric Equation"} :step/id #uuid "a2b6d468-dc59-53d1-b89f-9fdbc94f428b" :step/math-academy-id 18604 :step/next {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186088505}} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186088505 :step/content {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186088852 :tutorial/content "We often see parametric curves defined in terms of the parameter $t$, such as the parametric curve given below:\n\n$$\nx=2 \\cos t, \\,\\,\\, y=3 \\sin t, \\quad 0 \\leq t < 2 \\pi\n$$\n\nHowever, we can also define parametric curves in terms of other variables. Another commonly used variable is $\\theta$. So, in terms of $\\theta$, the above parametric equation is expressed as\n\n$$\nx=2 \\cos \\theta, \\,\\,\\, y=3 \\sin \\theta, \\quad 0 \\leq \\theta < 2 \\pi\n$$\n\nWe often use $\\theta$ when the bounds are\n\n$$\n0 \\leq \\theta < 2\\pi\n$$\n\nbecause this implies that the parameter $\\theta$ might be interpreted as an angle." :tutorial/id #uuid "5a132530-6b5f-59d1-bd02-6ac362b558a7" :tutorial/math-academy-id 3370 :tutorial/title "Defining Parametric Curves Using Other Parameters"} :step/id #uuid "35535a0b-1ada-5085-afad-0de6b32bfb5b" :step/math-academy-id 18844 :step/next {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186088531}} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186088531 :step/content {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186087673 :knowledge-point/canonical-example {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186087477} :knowledge-point/id #uuid "cddc940d-2185-569c-a62b-b507e95d3c35" :knowledge-point/key-prerequisites [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186049856}] :knowledge-point/questions [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186087970} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186088225}] :knowledge-point/title "Determining the Range of a Parametric Equation Defined in Terms of Trigonometric Functions"} :step/id #uuid "a097ab21-0ae3-5fba-a879-051b3ab0e156" :step/math-academy-id 19530}] :activity/title "Graphing Curves Defined Parametrically"}]}
